<speak>
<p>We have three polynomials, c of P, d of P, and g of P, built from the same four sign polynomials, H zero through H three. Let us use that common origin to build a polynomial E of P whose coefficients all become one modulo one plus i. For u equal to three, our target is one plus P, through P to the eighth. Call that all ones polynomial J of P.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>We want E of P to connect two parts of the proof. Expressing it through c, d, and g lets us use their comparisons later. Expressing it through the H polynomials will reveal its coefficient residues.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>The combination we choose is d of P minus c of P, divided by two, plus g of P minus c of P, divided by one plus i. Let us see why adding these terms gives the all ones congruence.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Substitute the definitions from Node three. The H zero terms cancel. The first quotient becomes minus the sum of H one of P and H three of P, divided by two.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>The second quotient becomes minus the sum of H two of P and H three of P, divided by two. Add i times the sum of H one of P and H two of P, divided by two.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Every half sum here has integer coefficients: adding two signs always gives an even integer. We form these exact quotients in the original ring, before reducing coefficients.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Now add the expressions. The two halves of negative H three of P combine into a whole. Collecting the other terms gives E of P equal to negative H three of P, plus the factor i minus one, multiplied by the half sum of H one of P and H two of P. This identity is the useful feature of our definition.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Reduce modulo one plus i. Since i minus one equals i times the sum one plus i, the entire second term disappears. We are left with the residue of negative H three of P.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Every remaining coefficient is positive or negative one. These signs have the same residue because their difference, two, is the product of one plus i and one minus i. Both signs therefore become one modulo one plus i.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Keep every power of P in place and replace its coefficient by one. In our nine position example, E of P is congruent to J of P: one plus P plus P squared, through P to the eighth. This is a congruence, not an equality of the original polynomials.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Finally substitute P equal to one. Each of the nine terms becomes one. Pair eight of those ones. Every pair is two, which vanishes modulo one plus i. One unpaired one remains. Thus E of one is congruent to J of one, which is nine, and nine is congruent to one.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>The same calculation works for general odd u. There are u squared terms in J of P, and u squared is odd. J of one equals u squared. Therefore E of one is congruent to one modulo one plus i. The common signs gave the all ones polynomial; the odd number of terms gives this final residue.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
